 **Parameters:**
-*   **Point-size / Footprint Radius:** Determines the spread of the splat [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Alpha-value:** Controls the opacity of individual voxels [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Gamma-value:** Applied to reduce contribution of points from tissues with differing densities [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Power Exponent:** Used in weighting calculations (default 4) [S1].
+*   **Point-size:** Diameter of the splat, can be fixed or distance-dependent [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Alpha-value:** Controls opacity blending [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Gamma-value:** Adjusts contrast for tissues with differing color value densities [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Power exponent:** Used in weighting calculations (typically 4) to reduce transition artifacts [S1].
 
 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Holes:** Discrete processing can produce holes on the periphery or during extreme perspective projection. This is countered by distributing energy across multiple pixels via a footprint table [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Aliasing:** Minor aliasing artifacts similar to non-anti-aliased triangle models may occur. GPU rasterization is not optimized for single-pixel quads, but performance is acceptable [S1].
-*   **Close-range Views:** At close range, voxel splats may appear as rectangular blocks covering many pixels, leading to pixelation artifacts induced by the finite resolution of RGB images [S1].
-*   **Brightness Variations:** Summing voxel kernels within volume slices most parallel to the image plane is prone to severe brightness variations in animated viewing [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Holes:** Discrete processing produces holes on the periphery or during extreme perspective projection. Mitigated by distributing energy across multiple pixels via the footprint table [S4, unofficial].
+*   **Blurring:** Tends to blur object edges and reduce image contrast [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Aliasing:** Minor aliasing artifacts similar to non-anti-aliased triangle models; close-range views show rectangular block artifacts and pixelation from finite RGB image resolution [S1].
+*   **Performance:** Requires extensive filtering and is time-consuming for large datasets if not optimized [S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Image-Space Sheet-Buffered Splatting Pipeline](8961-image-space-sheet-buffered-splatting-pipeline.md)
-A GPU-based variant that processes voxels in slabs parallel to the image plane, performing bucket construction, transformation, and depth sorting entirely on the GPU to avoid CPU-GPU memory transfer bottlenecks. Unlike CPU-based buckets containing all intersecting voxels, GPU buckets contain depth-sorted voxels whose centers fall inside a specific slab, allowing each voxel to belong to exactly one bucket while still being splatted for all intersecting slabs. Accelerates rendering by 2 to 2.5 times compared to CPU-based implementations.
+A GPU-accelerated variant that processes voxels in slabs parallel to the image plane, performing bucket construction and depth sorting on the GPU to avoid CPU-GPU bottlenecks, offering 2-2.5x speedup over CPU-based bucketing.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(W \cdot H)$ for the image buffer, plus $O(N)$ for the voxel data [G].
-*   **Performance:** Enables real-time interactivity due to hardware-accelerated rasterization [S1][S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial]. Performance is acceptable even for single-pixel quads, though not optimal [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Simple and efficient method for displaying contours and volume-oriented views. Allows interactive selection of voxelsets and fine-tuning of parameters [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Discrete processing can cause holes and aliasing. Close-range views suffer from pixelation and block artifacts [S1][S3, unofficial]. Tends to blur the edges of objects and reduce image contrast [S3, unofficial]. Requires extensive filtering, making it time-consuming [S3, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** Real-time interactive visualization of volumetric datasets, especially when user interaction (selection, parameter tuning) is required [S2, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** Not given in the sources.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of voxels $N$ and the number of pixels covered by each splat $P$. Total complexity $O(N \cdot P)$.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for voxel storage plus $O(W \cdot H)$ for the image buffer and footprint table, where $W, H$ are image dimensions.
+*   **Performance:** Achieves interactive frame rates (e.g., 1.1 Hz on a 150MHz R4400 for a $256^3$ volume with pre-processing) [S3, unofficial]. GPU implementations are significantly faster, though rasterization of single-pixel quads is not always optimized [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Interactive value selection; efficient culling of irrelevant voxels; suitable for parallelization based on object space subdivision [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Blurs edges; prone to brightness variations in animation (unless image-aligned splatting is used); discrete processing artifacts at extreme perspectives [S3, unofficial][S4, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** Interactive visualization, large datasets where pre-processing is acceptable, and when surface-oriented or volume-oriented views with interactive parameter tuning are required [S2, unofficial].
+*   **When not to use:** When high-fidelity edge preservation is critical, or when extreme perspective projection causes significant discretization artifacts that cannot be mitigated by footprint size [S3, unofficial][S4, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Voxel Splatting was first published in [20] (referenced in [S4, unofficial]). It is an object-space front-to-back algorithm where voxels are projected onto the screen as contributions that are highest at the center and drop off away from it [S4, unofficial][S5, unofficial]. The method is part of the broader splatting family, which treats volumetric data as a set of geometric primitives [S1][S3, unofficial][S4, unofficial]. It has been used in various applications for interactive visualization of volumetric data, including medical imaging and scientific visualization [S2, unofficial].
+Voxel Splatting was first published by Westover [S5, unofficial]. It represents the volume as an array of overlapping basis functions (commonly Gaussian kernels) scaled by voxel values, projecting them to the screen [S3, unofficial]. The method was introduced to address the aliasing and sampling issues of ray casting while providing interactive performance. Variations include Mueller's image-aligned splatting, which processes voxel kernels within slabs aligned parallel to the image plane to eliminate brightness variations in animation [S3, unofficial].
 
